  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Latest analysis this morning shows WNW to W low\nlevel flow on the southern periphery of a broad mid-level \ncyclone swirling over southeastern Canada. At the surface, high \npressure that has been in control for the past couple days is \nbeginning to weaken as it gives way to an approaching cold front\ncurrently draped across the Midwest. Low-level thicknesses will\nbuild today, aided by the aforementioned downsloping flow, will\naid in temperatures rising above climo into the low to mid 80s.\nCloser to the coast, temperatures will be tempered a few \ndegrees by a thick cirrus shield being pulled off of TC Isaias \nover the central Gulf.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The pattern begins to change after today as an \nupper level ridge shifts east from the TN Valley to the \nCarolinas. Meanwhile, a cutoff low is forecast to develop over \neast Texas. The flow between these two features should help to \nsteer TC Isaias north through the Gulf and towards the Gulf \nCoast, making landfall in the vicinity of Mobile Bay late\ntomorrow evening. \n\nEast of Isaias, a plume of tropical moisture, with anomalous \n2\"+ PWATs, is forecast to surge north through the southeastern\nCONUS over the weekend. The moisture, in tandem with enhanced \nlow- level convergence and strengthening WAA along a developing \nwarm front will support a period of moderate to heavy rain late\nSaturday into Sunday across all of ENC. Guidance continue to \nshow a solid signal for around 1-2\" of rain across much of the \narea. Average amounts have changed little, but probabilities for\nhigher-end amounts continue to trend upward. This is especially\ntrue along the coast where frictional convergence will likely\nenhanced rainfall rates. NBM probabilities for amounts higher\nthan 3\" are now between 50-60% here, and above 4\" probabilities\nare up to roughly 20-30% - only slightly higher than similar\nodds from Euro and GFS ensembles. \n\n6hr Flash flood guidance is around 2-4\" for the areas of ENC \nthat saw heavy rainfall a few days ago, while the rest of the \narea is 4\"+. With area averages forecast to fall below these \nranges, and over a longer period of time, the risk of \nsignificant hydro impacts appears low. That said, any convection\nthat develops will be capable of intense rainfall rates, with \nrate-driven flooding of poor drainage and urban areas possible.\nAreas from New Bern south remain in a Slight (Level 2 of 4) risk\nof excessive rainfall from WPC.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Steady increase in moisture through the weekend\npeaks on Sunday contributing to an increase in instability\nacross portions of ENC, greatest south of Highway 264. Factors\nlike cloud cover will determine how much we end up getting, but\nensemble averages suggest CAPEs as high as 1000+ J/kg in some\nspots. While low- level forcing is in question, it's a setup \nthat bears watching for a conditional severe thunderstorm risk.\nMachine learning guidance and EFI guidance have changed little\nfrom yesterday, still suggesting at least a marginal threat. \n\nThe remnants of Isaias are forecast to round the upper level \nridge and move off the Northern Mid-Atlantic Coast early next \nweek. Drier air then moves in behind it with a lower chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms by Tuesday.",
